othing to do  with riker looming in the background  instead it was just the disappointing  way tripp died blowing himself up to  take out three randos  as trenir told trek today i've gotten  out of much worse scrapes than that  malcolm mcdowell played one of the most  important villains in star trek history  as dr tullian soren in star trek  generations he was the force that united  jean-luc picard and james t kirk he was  also the man who kinda killed kirk using  a delayed response bridge collapse as it  turns out mcdowell thought that was  pretty stupid in 2011 he told  startrek.com if you have this icon of  american television why the hell didn't  they give him a spectacular death why  did they give him such a really paltry  death me shooting the bridge out or some  bs whatever it was  they should have sent him off in a  glorious fashion and they didn't  the scene originally had soren shoot  kirk in the back before reshoots  mcdowell also found this disappointing  and he lamented that they couldn't  figure out a better death he told metro  it was feeble i thought because i  thought he deserved better  william shatner has spent decades as one  of the faces of star trek and when  you're the head honcho of the uss  enterprise this comes with many great  moments but it also comes with many  deeply embarrassing ones  some of these were a product of the time  period others were a product of  shatner's own ego but the scene he  publicly regrets most is his final scene  when asked by crave online whether he  was satisfied with kirk's death in star  trek generations shatner replied no  no i would have done something else  according to shatner the suits at  paramount figured the star trek  franchise might make a lot more money if  they included the next generation cast  they also allegedly reasoned that there  wasn't much milk left in the original  series cash cow so it was a perfect time  for kirk to meet picard and they also  wanted to kill off kirk in order to  raise the stakes and signal a permanent  changing of the guard shatner told  trekmovie.com that he was presented with  two options it was either i was going to  appear and die or they were going to say  he died so i chose the more practical of  the two between the lackluster reviews  and the pathetic death shatner wasn't  happy he said i wish that there had been  more trumpets for the death of the  character  fun  as [ __ ] leonard nimoy was there for many  of star trek's greatest moments it's  easy to picture him performing a vulcan  nerf pincher explaining how the needs of  the many outweigh the needs of the few  but he was also front and center for one  of trek's worst hours spock's brain an  episode that was deeply embarrassing for  him  though it aired a half century ago  perhaps no episode has come to symbolize  trek at its worst more than spock's  brain  the episode sees spock's brain  surgically removed and kidnapped by an  alien species vulcan physiology lets  their bodies stay alive for 24 hours  without a brain so kirk goes hunting for  it  even for 60 scifi that was a bridge too  far in his autobiography i am spock  nimoy singled it out as a particularly  painful memory writing  frankly during the entire shooting of  that episode i was embarrassed a feeling  that overcame me many times during the  final season of star trek  though deep space nine is considered the  gritty star trek it still had a fair  number of off-putting moments including  some from no-nonsense commander benjamin  cisco when asked at a convention what  his least favorite scenes were avery  brooke said it was a toss-up between two  choices the first was move along home in  a scene that even the deep space nine  documentary what we left behind called  out as the worst scene in the series  he knew right away that he'd never live  it down saying there's nothing wrong  with the melody it's the hopscotch part  the second was apocalypse rising in  which he played a klingon which involved  enough prosthetics that it necessitated  a dentist appointment but while he  didn't want to do it brooks had no  choice he said the resistance you  observe with cisco playing a klingon was  real  dr julian bashir initially received some  backlash from deep space nine fans he  was seen by some as boring brash and  worst of all kind of annoying but while  the producers had a plan to make him  more interesting they didn't run it by  actor alexander siddig first the episode  dr bashir i presume reveals that bashir  was genetically modified as a child  which made him much smarter but siddig  didn't learn about this plot development  until the day before shooting he was  upset that the producers wouldn't go  over a major character arc with the  actor actually playing the character so  he staged his own personal protest he  told trekmovie.com  i did it the only way that an actor can  i completely destroyed the lines that  they gave me regarding the situation he  tanked the storyline putting no effort  into it going so far as to pin the lines  up on set so he could just read them  instead of memorize them  it might have been petty but it was  probably pretty satisfying nonetheless  few star trek characters were more  grounded than next generation's dr  beverly crusher except of course that  one time she fell in love with a ghost  in a magic lamp sub rosa is often listed  as one of the worst episodes of the next  generation an attempt at gothic romance  in space the story in somewhat reductive  terms involves beverly falling in love  with a ghost inhabiting a lamp it's  worth mentioning that this lamp ghost  has also seduced several generations of  her family needless to say actress gates  mcfadden found the whole concept absurd  in 2012 she told the crowd at austin  comic con i was reading this going this  woman became a doctor and she's in love  with a lamp i love lamp  do you really love the lamp are you just  saying it because you saw it  i love lamp  i love lamp  armin shimmerman is best known as quark  on deep space nine but years before ds9  was ever conceived he had a part as a  frankie on the next generation not just  any ferengi either but one of the first  ones ever put to screen and he regrets  every second of it the ferengi were  introduced in the last outpost just a  few episodes into the new series the  ferengi were meant to be menacing  threatening and anything but funny  nonetheless they came across as comical  and the episode is held in low regard to  this day their credibility as villains  tanked and they were mostly comic relief  until deep space nine shimmerman blamed  himself telling gamespot  no one bears the brunt of that mistake  more than i do so shimmerman approached  quark as an attempt to salvage the  ferengi all of my work on deep space  nine for the first four seasons was me  trying to eradicate that original  performance from everyone's mind  one of the all-time great comedic  actresses teri garr has played in  classics like tootsie and young  frankenstein she also played roberta  lincoln in assignment earth the season 2  finale of the original series it was one  of her first speaking parts and she  later credited it in her autobiography  with getting her real roles that doesn't  mean she liked it or even wants to talk  about it though gene roddenberry  intended assignment earth to be a  backdoor pilot to a new series hedging  his bets in case star trek got cancelled  ultimately star trek got a third season  and assignment earth remained a  standalone episode but years later when  interviewed for star log magazine gar  said i did that years ago and i mostly  deny i ever did it exactly why gar  disliked the experience so much as  unclear though producer bob justman said  in his book inside star trek that gene  roddenberry pushed to have gar wear a  shorter and more revealing skirt  whatever happened it was enough to sour  her on the whole franchise and fan base  she was glad assignment earth never  launched saying  otherwise all i would get would be star  trek questions for the rest of my  natural life and probably my unnatural  life you ever see those people who are  star trek fans the same people who go to  swap mates  you'd be forgiven for missing the fact  that jeffrey dean morgan appeared as an  alien in the enterprise episode  carpenter street as he was completely  buried in makeup and he hated the entire  experience  in 2003 morgan's career wasn't doing so  well and he needed work so he took a  role in enterprise he expected to do  some line readings instead he got four  hours a day in a makeup chair he told  entertainment weekly i remember them  dripping goop on my face and i had straw  sticking out of my nose i couldn't eat  lunch i was claustrophobic i'd go home  in tears the whole process was so  miserable he almost quit acting luckily  he didn't and the job helped him stay  afloat until better things came along  don't threaten me with a good time  garrett wang doesn't openly regret many  scenes he played as ensign harry kim  rather he regrets the scenes he didn't  get to play namely any as a senior  officer wang still takes umbrage with  the fact that harry remained an ensign  throughout star trek voyager telling  star trek.com i mean come on people kim  was probed beaten tortured and held the  distinction of being the first voyager  crew member to die and come back to life  what more does a guy have to do to get  promoted to lieutenant for frack's sake  wang called writer producer brennan  braga during the fourth season to ask  why harry hadn't been promoted he was  told quote well somebody's gotta be the  ensign check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite sci-fi  franchises are coming soon subscribe to  our youtube channel and hit the bell so  you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

